    "message": {
     "content": "This is a **solid opening offer** for a Google L6 Product Manager, but **you should definitely negotiate rather than accept as-is or walk away**. \n\nBecause you are well-positioned at Meta and were passively recruited, you hold strong leverage.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Breaking Down the Real Numbers\n\nGoogle\u2019s equity structure differs significantly from Meta\u2019s. Google uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule** for new-hire equity grants (typically **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** over 4 years).\n\n#### Year-by-Year Value of Google's Offer:\n* **Year 1:** $235,000 (Base) + $47,000 (Bonus @ 20%) + $356,400 (33% RSU) = **~$638,400**\n* **Year 2:** $235,000 + $47,000 + $356,400 = **~$638,400**\n* **Year 3:** $235,000 + $47,000 + $237,600 (22% RSU) = **~$519,600** *(+ whatever stacks from Refresher 1)*\n* **Year 4:** $235,000 + $47,000 + $129,600 (12% RSU) = **~$411,600** *(+ whatever stacks from Refreshers 1 & 2)*\n\n**Comparison to Meta:**\n* **Years 1 & 2:** You gain a significant jump over your current ~$526k/yr comp (+~$112k/yr).\n* **Years 3 & 4 (The \"Google Cliff\"):** Your initial grant drops sharply. While annual refreshers (which vest over 4 years at 25%/yr) will offset part of this drop, Meta\u2019s refresher and performance multipliers tend to be more aggressive, meaning long-term compensation parity requires steady performance at Google.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Where the Offer Falls Short (Your Leverage)\n\n1. **Base Salary is a Step Down:** They offered **$235k**, which is lower than your current **$240k** Meta base. The posted band reaches up to **$271k**, leaving plenty of headroom.\n2. **Zero Sign-On Bonus:** It is standard practice in Big Tech for an external L6 PM recruit from Meta to receive a sign-on bonus. This offsets unvested equity, forgone bonus cycles, and moving friction.\n3. **Passive Candidate Position:** Google reached out to you. You do not have to leave, which gives you maximum pricing power.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Ask For (Target Numbers)\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Target Counter-Offer | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$250,000 \u2013 $255,000** | Matches/beats current base; sits well inside the $271k maximum. |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Offsets leaving Meta mid-cycle and bridges transition risk. |\n| **Equity (RSUs)** | $1,080,000 / 4 yrs | **$1,200,000 / 4 yrs** | Strengthens total comp and cushions the Year 3/4 drop. |\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Frame the Negotiation\n\nReach out to your recruiter via phone or email with a collaborative, appreciative, and clear stance:\n\n> *\"Thank you for walking me through the offer. I've really enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m excited about the scope of this role.*\n>\n> *As mentioned earlier, I wasn\u2019t actively looking and have strong momentum at Meta where I'm currently at $240k base and ~$526k total target compensation with $200k in unvested equity vesting over the next year. Taking on the risk of switching teams and walking away from that trajectory needs to make clear financial sense.*\n>\n> *To make this a compelling move:*\n> 1. *Given my current base is $240k and the posted band goes up to $271k, I'd like to get base salary to **$250,000**.*\n> 2. *To bridge the unvested equity and bonus timing I\u2019d forfeit at Meta, I would need a **sign-on bonus of $75,000 to $100,000**.*\n> 3. *To ensure strong total comp continuity through Years 3 and 4, I'd like to see the initial equity grant bumped to **$1.2M**.*\n>\n> *If we can get close to these numbers, I\u2019m ready to sign.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Bottom Line\n* **Do not walk away:** The initial offer already beats your current cash flow for Years 1\u20132.\n* **Do not accept yet:** Google rarely gives their top-of-band numbers on the first pass.\n* **Negotiate firmly:** Securing a sign-on bonus and a base bump is standard for L6 transitions from Meta and will make the switch strictly accretive.",
